@startuml
' ============================================================================
' METADATA
'   author:       TheRobotDrafts Examples
'   description:  Side-by-side variant — the same 3-service workload deployed
'                 onto bare VMs (left) vs deployed as containers on a host
'                 running a container runtime (right).
'   usecase:      Compare deployment topologies and their node modeling.
'   complexity:   medium
'   domain:       deployment
' ============================================================================
title Example Deployment Diagram - Variant: Containers vs VMs

' ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
' This variant shows ONE workload deployed TWO ways. The goal is to teach
' how the SAME running software is modeled differently depending on the
' substrate, and to make the trade-offs visible on a single page.
'
' LEFT frame  — "Bare VM" approach:
'   Each service runs on its own VM. Every VM is a <<device>> (a virtual
'   machine is modeled like a physical box) hosting an OS modeled as an
'   <<executionEnvironment>>, with the app binary deployed on top.
'
' RIGHT frame — "Containers on a host" approach:
'   One physical/virtual host runs a container runtime. Each service is a
'   container — modeled as an artifact deployed onto the runtime, NOT as a
'   separate node. The runtime is the <<executionEnvironment>>; the host
'   is the <<device>>.
'
' Key modeling rule:
'   A VM gets its OWN node (it has its own kernel). A container does NOT —
'   it shares the host kernel, so it is an artifact inside the runtime node.
' ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

' ======================= LEFT: BARE VMs ===================================
frame "Approach A: Bare VMs (one VM per service)" as vms {

  ' Each VM is a full machine -> its own `node`, with an OS as an
  ' execution environment and the app deployed on top.
  node "VM: Web" as vmWeb <<device>> {
    artifact "Debian 12" as osW <<executionEnvironment>>
    artifact "web-2.0.0.jar" as webW <<deploy>>
  }
  node "VM: API" as vmApi <<device>> {
    artifact "Debian 12" as osA <<executionEnvironment>>
    artifact "api-2.0.0.jar" as apiA <<deploy>>
  }
  node "VM: Worker" as vmWorker <<device>> {
    artifact "Debian 12" as osK <<executionEnvironment>>
    artifact "worker-2.0.0.jar" as workerA <<deploy>>
  }
}

' ======================= RIGHT: CONTAINERS ================================
frame "Approach B: Containers on one host" as ctr {

  ' ONE host machine (device). The container runtime is the execution
  ' environment that sits on top of the OS.
  node "Host Machine" as host <<device>> {
    artifact "Ubuntu 22.04" as hostos <<executionEnvironment>>

    ' The runtime is ITSELF an execution environment that hosts containers.
    artifact "containerd 1.7" as runtime <<executionEnvironment>> {

      ' Containers are artifacts, NOT nodes — they share the host kernel.
      ' Model each container as a <<deploy>> artifact inside the runtime.
      artifact "web:2.0.0\n(container)" as webC <<deploy>>
      artifact "api:2.0.0\n(container)" as apiC <<deploy>>
      artifact "worker:2.0.0\n(container)" as workerC <<deploy>>
    }
  }
}

' --- Inter-service wiring (identical in BOTH approaches) -----------------
' The LOGICAL topology is the same: web -> api -> worker. Only the physical
' substrate differs. Showing both makes that point land.
webW --> apiA : HTTP (intra-VPC)
apiA --> workerA : AMQP

webC --> apiC : HTTP (loopback)
apiC --> workerC : AMQP

' --- Comparison notes -----------------------------------------------------
note bottom of vms
  BARE VMs
  --------
  + Strong isolation (separate kernels)
  + Simple networking (each VM = 1 IP)
  - Heavy: 3 full OS images to patch
  - Slow to start / scale (minutes)
  - One artifact per VM by design
end note

note bottom of ctr
  CONTAINERS ON A HOST
  --------------------
  + Lightweight (shared kernel)
  + Fast start / scale (seconds)
  + Dense packing (many per host)
  - Weaker isolation (shared kernel)
  - Host kernel exploit = all containers
  - Needs a runtime (containerd / Docker)
  Modeling rule: container = artifact,
  NOT a node — it has no own kernel.
end note

' Recap:
'   * VM approach  -> 3 nodes, each a <<device>> with its own OS.
'   * Container    -> 1 host node; runtime is an <<executionEnvironment>>;
'     each container is a <<deploy>> artifact nested inside it.
'   * Nested artifact blocks (runtime { artifact, artifact }) show the
'     "containers live INSIDE the runtime" relationship visually.
'   * The LOGICAL call graph (web -> api -> worker) is identical on both
'     sides — deployment topology and service topology are ORTHOGONAL.

@enduml
